  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Down four after one inning, the Yankees chased the entire game and never caught up.",
    "what_this_means": "Beck's early exit. 3.2 innings, five runs. Left no margin for error, and Minnesota expanded the lead to seven by the eighth. Schuemann kept them breathing with a two-run homer, and they scraped together three runs in the fifth to make it close, but left eight runners on base across a game that turned on missed moments in the second, sixth, and eighth innings. One of eleven RISP chances converted into a run is not an offensive profile. That's a locked door. The bullpen held after Beck departed. Hill, Bird, Yarbrough, Doval, and Headrick combined for 4.2 scoreless frames. But the early damage was structural. Once Minnesota established a lead that wide that early, the Yankees were fighting against their own deficit, not their opponent. They had enough moments to imagine winning. They didn't have the execution to finish it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Joe Ryan takes the mound for Minnesota tonight at 1:35 PM with a 3.61 ERA and the Twins already holding a four-game lead. Ryan Weathers starts for the Yankees and cannot afford another early collapse.",
